rials.\n- The relationship between the raw material properties and the response variable should be linear or appropriately modeled.\n- Independence of observations is assumed, meaning that the effect of one experimental run does not influence another.\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria**  \nRaw materials should be considered in experimental designs when their properties are expected to significantly influence the outcome of the study. Selection criteria include the relevance of the raw material characteristics to the product performance and the feasibility of controlling or measuring these properties during the experiment.\n\n**Analysis Procedure**  \nThe analysis of experiments involving raw materials typically involves ANOVA to assess the significance of the effects of raw material properties on the response variable. If multiple factors are involved, factorial ANOVA or regression analysis may be employed to understand interactions and main effects.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths**  \nUtilizing raw materials in experimental designs allows for a more realistic simulation of production processes. It enables researchers to identify optimal material properties that enhance product performance, leading to improved quality and efficiency in manufacturing.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages**  \nThe primary limitations include the potential variability in raw material properties, which can introduce noise into the experimental results. Additionally, the complexity of interactions between multiple raw material characteristics may complicate the analysis and interpretation of results.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives**  \nRelated designs include factorial designs, response surface methodology, and mixture designs, which can all incorporate raw material properties as factors. Alternatives may involve using surrogate materials or simplified models if raw material variability is too high.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations**  \nWhen i